{
  "name": "Law Firm AI Audit: How to Inventory Your AI Tools",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1: It's easy to lose track of all the AI tools popping up in your law…",
      "text": "It's easy to lose track of all the AI tools popping up in your law firm, right? From generative AI for research to predictive analytics in case management, these tools can really streamline things. But with convenience comes responsibility, especially when client data is involved. Knowing exactly which AI tools your team uses and how they fit into your workflows is the very first step toward responsible AI adoption. This isn't just about compliance; it's about protecting your clients and your firm's reputation.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 2: First, you'll want to map out where AI is showing up",
      "text": "First, you'll want to map out where AI is showing up. Think about any software that automates tasks, generates content, or analyzes data. This means more than just the obvious ChatGPT; it includes features embedded in your existing legal tech. Next, dig into what kind of client or matter data each tool processes. Is it sensitive? Confidential? Understanding the data flow is critical for risk assessment. Then, clarify whether outputs from these tools are actually reviewed by a lawyer before being used externally. Human oversight is key to catching errors or biases. Finally, don't forget to check vendor agreements: do they retain prompts or use your firm's data for model training? Many do, and that can be a huge confidentiality issue. Taking a systematic approach helps you identify blind spots and understand your firm's true AI landscape.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 2
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 3: What to Prepare Before You Start: Before diving into an AI audit, g…",
      "text": "What to Prepare Before You Start: Before diving into an AI audit, gather a list of all software and platforms your firm uses, even if you’re unsure if they contain AI features. This might include research tools, document review software, case management systems, and even internal communication platforms. Have copies of vendor contracts or terms of service handy. Knowing who uses what and for what purpose will make the inventory process much smoother and more effective.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 3
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 4: Ready to get a handle on your firm's AI usage?",
      "text": "Ready to get a handle on your firm's AI usage? Let's chat about how an AI audit can bring clarity and security to your operations.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 4
    }
  ],
